There are constants \(	heta_0>0,C<\infty\), depending only on \(m\) and the two fixed Schwartz functions, with the following properties. For \(0<|	heta|<	heta_0\), set \[ \epsilon=\operatorname{sign}	heta,\quad y=|	heta|^{1/m},\quad 
u=y^{mn_{\mathrm f}}=|	heta|^{n_{\mathrm f}}, \] and define the norm-convergent series  egin{equation} K_	heta=\sum_{\gamma\in L} 
u J_\epsilon(\Delta_y\gamma)u_	heta(\gamma). \end{equation} It is self-adjoint and satisfies, uniformly for both signs,  egin{align} \|K_	heta\|_{\mathrm{coeff},1}&\le C,& \|K_	heta\|_{\mathrm{coeff},2}&\asymp\sqrt{
u}, \ \|K_	heta^2-K_	heta\|_{\mathrm{coeff},1}&\le Cy,& \|K_	heta^2-K_	heta\|_{\mathrm{coeff},2}&\le Cy\sqrt{
u}. \end{align} Continuous functional calculus in \(C_r^*(L,	heta c)\) gives a projection \(p_	heta\) with  egin{equation} \|p_	heta-K_	heta\|\le Cy,\qquad 0<\operatorname{tr}_	heta(p_	heta)\le C|	heta|^{n_{\mathrm f}}. \end{equation} These fields are measurable on the intervals \(0<|	heta|<	heta_0\).
